WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Gates and circuits.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.369
If you know matrix multiplication, you already have the machinery.

3
00:00:06.369 --> 00:00:08.033
Only the vocabulary is new.

4
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:11.245
A quantum program is a circuit.

5
00:00:11.245 --> 00:00:16.500
It is a sequence of unitary matrices applied to a state, ending in a measurement.

6
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.700
Unitary means reversible and length preserving.

7
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.467
Here are the gates worth knowing.

8
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.583
X, Y and Z flip states or change phase.

9
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:27.100
X is the quantum NOT.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.334
Hadamard creates superposition.

11
00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:32.267
It is the first move of nearly every algorithm.

12
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.836
The rotation gates turn by any angle.

13
00:00:34.836 --> 00:00:39.067
Those angles become trainable parameters in quantum machine learning.

14
00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:41.442
And CNOT entangles two qubits.

15
00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:46.700
Without it, a circuit is just independent qubits that a laptop can simulate.

16
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.600
Let's build the classic circuit.

17
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.533
Both qubits start at zero.

18
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.367
Hadamard puts the first one in superposition.

19
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.800
CNOT entangles the second with it.

20
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.633
Measure, and you see zero zero or one one, about half each.

21
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:03.200
Here it is in Qiskit.

22
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.467
The h call puts qubit zero in superposition.

23
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.857
The cx call is CNOT, entangling qubit one.

24
00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:13.536
On real hardware you will also see stray outcomes.

25
00:01:13.536 --> 00:01:14.567
That is noise.

26
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:17.115
Now the algorithms.

27
00:01:17.115 --> 00:01:21.300
Grover searches N unstructured items in about root N steps.

28
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.491
That is a quadratic gain, not exponential, and it needs the search encoded as an oracle.

29
00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:29.633
It does not make databases fast.

30
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:32.000
A few more by name.

31
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.342
Shor factors large integers, and breaks RSA.

32
00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:39.767
It needs a large fault tolerant machine that does not exist yet.

33
00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:44.700
VQE finds molecule energies, the most credible near term application.

34
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
QAOA targets optimization, but classical solvers still win.

35
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.333
HHL solves linear systems, with caveats that destroy most claimed uses.

36
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:56.345
Let's recap.

37
00:01:56.345 --> 00:02:00.000
A circuit is unitary matrices followed by measurement.

38
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.267
Hadamard superposes, and CNOT entangles.

39
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.767
Grover is only a quadratic speedup.

40
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.819
Shor and VQE matter most.

41
00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:10.833
Next up: noise, NISQ, and error correction.
